    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/as-pure-c/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Use the standard formula for expanding (1 + 2𝑥)𝑛. (1 + 2𝑥)𝑛= 1 + 𝑛(2𝑥) + 𝑛(𝑛−1) (2𝑥)2 + 𝑛(𝑛−1)(𝑛−2) (2𝑥)3 + ⋯ [M1] 2! 3! (1 + 2𝑥)7 = 1 + 7(2𝑥) + 7 × 6 1 × 2(2𝑥)2 + 7 × 6 × 5 1 × 2 × 3(2𝑥)3 + ⋯ [A1] [A1] = 1 + 14𝑥+ 21(4𝑥2) + 35(8𝑥3) + ⋯ = 1 + 14𝑥+ 84𝑥2 + 280𝑥3 + ⋯. [A1] (b) Factorise the quadratic and use part (a): (3 + 4𝑥−4𝑥2)(1 + 2𝑥)6 = −(4𝑥2 −4𝑥−3)(1 + 2𝑥)6 [M1] = −(2𝑥+ 1)(2𝑥−3)(1 + 2𝑥)6\" style=\"--madas-width:608.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/as-pure-c/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"= −(2𝑥−3)(2𝑥+ 1)7 = (3 −2𝑥)(1 + 2𝑥)7 [A1] = (3 −2𝑥)(1 + 14𝑥+ 84𝑥2 + 280𝑥3 + ⋯) [M1] = 3 + 42𝑥+ 252𝑥2 + 840𝑥3 + ⋯ −2𝑥−28𝑥2 −168𝑥3 + ⋯ [A1] = 3 + 40𝑥+ 224𝑥2 + 672𝑥3 + ⋯. [A1] Alternative to part (b): (1 + 2𝑥)6 = 1 + 6(2𝑥) + 6 × 5 (2𝑥)2 + 6 × 5 × 4 (2𝑥)3 + ⋯ 2! 3! = 1 + 12𝑥+ 15(4𝑥2) + 20(8𝑥3) + ⋯ = 1 + 12𝑥+ 60𝑥2 + 160𝑥3 + ⋯. Multiply by the given quadratic:\" style=\"--madas-width:605.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/as-pure-c/q11-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(3 + 4𝑥−4𝑥2)(1 + 2𝑥)6 = (3 + 4𝑥−4𝑥2)(1 + 12𝑥+ 60𝑥2 + 160𝑥3 + ⋯) = 3 + 36𝑥+ 180𝑥2 + 480𝑥3 + ⋯ + 4𝑥+ 48𝑥2 + 240𝑥3 + ⋯ −4𝑥2 −48𝑥3 + ⋯ = 3 + 40𝑥+ 224𝑥2 + 672𝑥3 + ⋯, as before.\" style=\"--madas-width:568.0px\"></figure>",
